#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:1
msgid "The unstructured level"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:3
msgid ""
"When a layout resists parameterisation, write it. One format, one Python "
"module under `content/algorithms/unstructured/`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:20
msgid ""
"The module exports a `pipelines` mapping. Two things about that example "
"matter more than the code itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:23
msgid "The contract is by shape, not by type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:25
msgid ""
"`pipelines` maps a name to **any object** with `pdf_extract`, "
"`text_filter` and `deserialize` attributes, each a callable or an "
"iterable of callables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:28
msgid ""
"The engine's own `Pipeline` satisfies that protocol, but a plain object —"
" or a dictionary — works just as well. Which is what lets a format be "
"written **without importing the engine at all**, and means a repository's"
" Python is testable on its own terms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:32
msgid "Native and author pipes mix inside one segment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:34
msgid ""
"The example does exactly that: a **library** pipe configured with a "
"selection **you** built."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:36
msgid ""
"A native pipe passed in from Python is unwrapped back to its Rust form "
"rather than being called through Python for every block. Only your own "
"callables stay wrapped, and they are the only thing that pays the "
"boundary cost."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:40
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"That has a visible consequence for speed: an author's pipe declares that "
"it does **not** scale with threads, so a page class handled by one runs "
"sequentially rather than contending for the GIL "
"({doc}`../../../reference/design/parallelism`). A format that gains "
"nothing from `--pages` is usually one of these, and that is the design "
"working."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:45
msgid "The classification finalizer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:47
msgid "A module may also export `compute_page_class`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:56
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"It receives the raw per-page classes of **one document** and returns the "
"resolved ones. This is where *\"this page continues the table that "
"started on the previous one\"* is expressed, and it is the only place in "
"the engine where reasoning across pages is available at all "
"({doc}`../../../reference/design/classification`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:61
msgid "What happens when your pipe raises"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/advanced/unstructured.md:62
msgid ""
"A Python exception raised by your pipe is logged **with its traceback** "
"and converted to a typed error at the boundary. No Python exception "
"travels further into the engine, so a format cannot crash a run: the page"
" your pipe raised on is skipped, with an error row naming the format, the"
" page and the pipe, and every other page of the document is extracted as "
"usual. A pipe that raises on every page therefore yields an empty result "
"and a log full of rows saying why, rather than a run that dies."
msgstr ""

